commons bynk.ordering {
	---
	How one value compares with another: `Less`, `Equal` or `Greater`. It is what
	`a.compare(b)` returns on every type that has an order, and what a `sortWith`
	comparator returns. Its own order is `Less < Equal < Greater`, so it can be
	stored, matched and compared like any enum.

	Its variants name an `Ordering` only where the context says so (a `match` or
	`is` on one, an expected type, the other side of `==`, `!=` or an ordering
	operator): a bare `Less` is never an `Ordering` on its own, so a type of your
	own with a `Less` variant is unaffected. `Ordering.Less` always works.
	---
	type Ordering = enum { Less, Equal, Greater } derives Ord

	---
	`next` when `self` is `Equal`, otherwise `self`: the way to break a tie, so
	`a.x.compare(b.x).then(a.y.compare(b.y))` orders by `x` and then by `y`.
	`next` is a value, evaluated either way, not a lambda.
	---
	fn Ordering.then(self, next: Ordering) -> Ordering {
		match self {
			Equal => next,
			Less => Ordering.Less,
			Greater => Ordering.Greater,
		}
	}

	---
	The opposite order: `Less` and `Greater` swap and `Equal` stays. Use it to
	sort one key in descending order inside a multi-key comparator.
	---
	fn Ordering.reverse(self) -> Ordering {
		match self {
			Less => Ordering.Greater,
			Equal => Ordering.Equal,
			Greater => Ordering.Less,
		}
	}
}
